        "title": "Government's Tribunals Bill Sparks Debate Over Creation of New Judicial Bodies",
        "slug": "governments-tribunals-bill-sparks-debate-over-creation-of-new-judicial-bodies",
        "category": "General news",
        "publishedDate": "2026-07-19",
        "readTime": "2m",
        "summary": "The proposed Tribunals Bill has generated public discussion over the need to establish new judicial institutions in Ghana. While proponents argue that tribunals could improve access to justice and speed up the resolution of cases, critics have questioned whether their functions overlap with powers already available within the existing court system.",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>The proposed Tribunals Bill has become one of the latest issues generating debate within Ghana&#39;s legal and political space, as stakeholders examine the need, structure, and possible impact of establishing new judicial bodies.<br>The bill is aimed at creating specialised tribunals with the mandate to handle particular categories of cases outside the traditional court system. Advocates of the proposal believe that specialised adjudication could help address delays within the justice system by allowing certain disputes to be handled more quickly and efficiently.<br>Supporters argue that tribunals can provide a more focused approach to resolving specific matters because members appointed to such bodies may have expertise in the areas they are expected to handle. They believe the move could improve access to justice, reduce the workload on regular courts, and ensure faster decisions on important cases.<br>However, the proposal has also attracted criticism, with some legal practitioners and commentators questioning whether the creation of new tribunals is necessary when Ghana&#39;s existing courts already have broad constitutional powers to administer justice.<br>Concerns have been raised about possible overlap between the responsibilities of the proposed tribunals and the existing judiciary. Critics argue that establishing new institutions could lead to duplication, increased government expenditure, and uncertainty over the relationship between tribunals and the courts.<br>There are also broader questions surrounding the independence, composition, and accountability of the proposed bodies. Some stakeholders have emphasised that any new judicial institution must operate transparently and maintain public confidence in the justice system.<br>The government, however, maintains that the proposed reforms are intended to strengthen Ghana&#39;s legal framework and improve the delivery of justice. Officials supporting the bill have argued that specialised tribunals are common in many jurisdictions and can complement, rather than replace, the work of existing courts.<br>As parliamentary discussions and public consultations continue, the Tribunals Bill is expected to remain a major topic of conversation among legal experts, lawmakers, and citizens who are closely watching its potential impact on Ghana&#39;s justice system.<br>Published by Orpheus Grant-Essilfie</p><img src=\"https://eu-west-2.graphassets.com/cmrmsa7zm00ij01wc3pwrgjxy/cmrs74hgxy77h07jpo923yt2g\" alt=\"1cd646_ae7f0376474742e4ac9a0dee2f3f5a5d~mv2_d_2508_1672_s_2.jpg\" title=\"1cd646_ae7f0376474742e4ac9a0dee2f3f5a5d~mv2_d_2508_1672_s_2.jpg\" width=\"568\" height=\"378\" /><p></p>",
          "raw": {
            "children": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The proposed Tribunals Bill has become one of the latest issues generating debate within Ghana's legal and political space, as stakeholders examine the need, structure, and possible impact of establishing new judicial bodies.\nThe bill is aimed at creating specialised tribunals with the mandate to handle particular categories of cases outside the traditional court system. Advocates of the proposal believe that specialised adjudication could help address delays within the justice system by allowing certain disputes to be handled more quickly and efficiently.\nSupporters argue that tribunals can provide a more focused approach to resolving specific matters because members appointed to such bodies may have expertise in the areas they are expected to handle. They believe the move could improve access to justice, reduce the workload on regular courts, and ensure faster decisions on important cases.\nHowever, the proposal has also attracted criticism, with some legal practitioners and commentators questioning whether the creation of new tribunals is necessary when Ghana's existing courts already have broad constitutional powers to administer justice.\nConcerns have been raised about possible overlap between the responsibilities of the proposed tribunals and the existing judiciary. Critics argue that establishing new institutions could lead to duplication, increased government expenditure, and uncertainty over the relationship between tribunals and the courts.\nThere are also broader questions surrounding the independence, composition, and accountability of the proposed bodies. Some stakeholders have emphasised that any new judicial institution must operate transparently and maintain public confidence in the justice system.\nThe government, however, maintains that the proposed reforms are intended to strengthen Ghana's legal framework and improve the delivery of justice. Officials supporting the bill have argued that specialised tribunals are common in many jurisdictions and can complement, rather than replace, the work of existing courts.\nAs parliamentary discussions and public consultations continue, the Tribunals Bill is expected to remain a major topic of conversation among legal experts, lawmakers, and citizens who are closely watching its potential impact on Ghana's justice system.\nPublished by Orpheus Grant-Essilfie"
