      {
        "title": "Chairman Wontumi Sentenced to 20 Years in Illegal Mining Case",
        "slug": "chairman-wontumi-sentenced-to-20-years-in-illegal-mining-case",
        "category": "General news",
        "publishedDate": "2026-07-20",
        "readTime": "2m",
        "summary": "The High Court in Accra has sentenced NPP Ashanti Regional Chairman Bernard Antwi Boasiako, popularly known as Chairman Wontumi, to 20 years' imprisonment after convicting him on two counts related to illegal mining activities. The prison terms will run concurrently, and the court also imposed fines on both Wontumi and Akonta Mining Limited.",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>The High Court in Accra has sentenced the Ashanti Regional Chairman of the New Patriotic Party (NPP), Bernard Antwi Boasiako, popularly known as Chairman Wontumi, to 20 years&#39; imprisonment after finding him guilty on two counts in the high-profile Akonta Mining illegal mining case.<br>Justice Audrey Kocuvie-Tay handed down 20-year prison terms on each of the two counts, ordering that the sentences run concurrently. The court also imposed a fine of 10,000 penalty units on each count, with an additional custodial sentence to apply in default of payment, as prescribed by law.<br>The court found Wontumi guilty of assigning mineral rights without the required ministerial approval and knowingly facilitating unlawful mining activities. According to the judgment, he unlawfully permitted mining operations to take place on Akonta Mining&#39;s concession without obtaining the necessary authorisation under Ghana&#39;s mining laws.<br>During the proceedings, the court dismissed several applications by the defence, including a request to halt the judgment and refer constitutional issues to the Supreme Court. The judge ruled that the relevant provisions of the Minerals and Mining Act were clear and did not require constitutional interpretation.<br></p><p><a title=\"https://www.facebook.com/reel/2180891935793857/?app=fbl\" href=\"https://www.facebook.com/reel/2180891935793857/?app=fbl\">https://www.facebook.com/reel/2180891935793857/?app=fbl</a></p><p>Watch video</p><p>Akonta Mining Limited, which was also standing trial, was convicted on two counts and fined 15,000 penalty units on each count. The company was found to have unlawfully assigned mineral rights and facilitated illegal mining operations.<br>The case arose from alleged illegal mining activities in the Samreboi area of the Western Region and has become one of Ghana&#39;s most significant prosecutions in the country&#39;s ongoing fight against illegal mining, commonly known as galamsey. The judgment is expected to have far-reaching legal and political implications given Chairman Wontumi&#39;s prominent position within the opposition NPP.<br>The defence is expected to challenge the conviction at the Court of Appeal. Until then, the High Court&#39;s judgment remains in force.<br>.</p>",
          "raw": {
            "children": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The High Court in Accra has sentenced the Ashanti Regional Chairman of the New Patriotic Party (NPP), Bernard Antwi Boasiako, popularly known as Chairman Wontumi, to 20 years' imprisonment after finding him guilty on two counts in the high-profile Akonta Mining illegal mining case.\nJustice Audrey Kocuvie-Tay handed down 20-year prison terms on each of the two counts, ordering that the sentences run concurrently. The court also imposed a fine of 10,000 penalty units on each count, with an additional custodial sentence to apply in default of payment, as prescribed by law.\nThe court found Wontumi guilty of assigning mineral rights without the required ministerial approval and knowingly facilitating unlawful mining activities. According to the judgment, he unlawfully permitted mining operations to take place on Akonta Mining's concession without obtaining the necessary authorisation under Ghana's mining laws.\nDuring the proceedings, the court dismissed several applications by the defence, including a request to halt the judgment and refer constitutional issues to the Supreme Court. The judge ruled that the relevant provisions of the Minerals and Mining Act were clear and did not require constitutional interpretation.\n"
