                    "text": "Akonta Mining Limited, which was also standing trial, was convicted on two counts and fined 15,000 penalty units on each count. The company was found to have unlawfully assigned mineral rights and facilitated illegal mining operations.\nThe case arose from alleged illegal mining activities in the Samreboi area of the Western Region and has become one of Ghana's most significant prosecutions in the country's ongoing fight against illegal mining, commonly known as galamsey. The judgment is expected to have far-reaching legal and political implications given Chairman Wontumi's prominent position within the opposition NPP.\nThe defence is expected to challenge the conviction at the Court of Appeal. Until then, the High Court's judgment remains in force.\n."
