        "summary": "The Ballon d’Or organisers have clarified that winning the FIFA World Cup can influence the voting process, but the prestigious award is ultimately decided by a player’s overall performances throughout the season.",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>The debate surrounding the importance of the FIFA World Cup in the Ballon d’Or race has received an official clarification from the organisers of football’s most prestigious individual award.<br>The official Ballon d’Or account released a statement explaining that while winning the World Cup can strengthen a player’s chances, it does not automatically make them the winner.<br>“Winning the World Cup helps, but it does not guarantee a Ballon d’Or. To win it, you need to have been the best player of the season, not only of the World Cup,” the statement read.</p><p><br>The clarification reinforces the idea that the Ballon d’Or is not decided by a single tournament but by a player’s complete body of work over an entire football season.</p><p><br>According to the organisers, voters consider several factors, including individual performances, consistency, goals and assists, influence on matches, leadership, and achievements with both club and country.</p><p><br>While the FIFA World Cup remains one of the biggest stages in football and can have a huge impact on public opinion, the award is designed to recognise the player who performs at the highest level throughout the year.<br>World Cup success and Ballon d’Or history<br>Winning the World Cup has often played a major role in the Ballon d’Or conversation. A strong performance on the global stage can create unforgettable moments and increase a player’s chances.<br>However, history has shown that World Cup winners do not always win the Ballon d’Or.</p><p><br>Several players have produced exceptional club seasons and dominated football discussions despite not lifting the World Cup trophy. </p><p>This demonstrates that the award is based on overall excellence rather than one competition alone.</p><p><br>The most recent examples have also sparked debates among fans, with many arguing whether international success should outweigh a player’s achievements across domestic leagues and European competitions.<br>Club performances remain a key factor<br>Modern football has placed even more importance on club performances because players compete in major competitions such as the UEFA Champions League, domestic leagues and cup competitions throughout the season.</p><p><br>A player who consistently performs at the highest level for their club, delivers in important matches and produces outstanding individual numbers can still have a strong claim for the Ballon d’Or without winning the World Cup.</p><p><br>The organisers’ message is clear: the Ballon d’Or is awarded to the best footballer of the season, not simply the winner of the biggest international trophy.<br>Debate continues among football fans.</p><p><br>The statement is expected to continue discussions among supporters, especially during years when major international tournaments take place.<br>Some fans believe the World Cup should have the biggest influence because it represents the highest level of international football.</p><p> Others argue that judging a player over an entire season gives a more complete picture of their quality and consistency.<br>Ultimately, the Ballon d’Or remains a balance between individual brilliance, team success and a player’s overall impact on football.</p><p><br>The official position from the organisers settles one point: winning the World Cup is a major advantage, but it is not a guaranteed path to winning the Ballon d’Or</p>",
