      {
        "title": "The Chronicles of Bernard Antwi Boasiako (Chairman Wontumi): An Editorial by Orpheus Grant-Essilfie",
        "slug": "the-chronicles-of-bernard-antwi-boasiako-chairman-wontumi-an-editorial-by-orpheus-grant-essilfie",
        "category": "General news",
        "publishedDate": "2026-07-21",
        "readTime": "8m read",
        "summary": "This comprehensive article examines the High Court judgment against Bernard Antwi Boasiako. It explores the specific reasons for his arrest, the sophisticated legal defense mounted by his attorney, the explosive political reactions, and the severe socioeconomic impact of a twenty-year prison sentence on the livelihoods of completely innocent employees.",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>​The Verdict and Its Core Premise<br>​On Monday, July 20, 2026, the Superior Court of Judicature in the High Court of Justice Criminal Court 4 held in Accra delivered a judgment that fundamentally altered the landscape of environmental law and political accountability.</p><p></p><p> Presided over by Her Ladyship Justice Audrey Kocuvie Tay, the court pronounced a guilty verdict against Bernard Antwi Boasiako, universally recognized as Chairman Wontumi, and his corporate vehicle, Akonta Mining Company Limited.</p><img src=\"https://eu-west-2.graphassets.com/cmrmsa7zm00ij01wc3pwrgjxy/cmruku7x42tr707mhg5tp6r80\" alt=\"SP.jpg\" title=\"SP.jpg\" width=\"257\" height=\"300\" /><p><br>​The fundamental premise of a just society is that the law must absolutely work and work blindly, regardless of party colors or wealth. However, while one must vigorously uphold the rule of law, one must also look critically at the proportionality of the punishment. </p><p>A twenty-year jail term in hard labor is exceptionally harsh. Bernard Antwi Boasiako has a massive chain of legitimate businesses across the country. Jailing him for two straight decades means the livelihood of thousands of completely innocent people is gone, creating a massive economic crisis for ordinary citizens.</p><p><strong><br>​The Operation and Arrest</strong><br>​The complex legal journey leading to this severe conviction began deep within the dense, restricted forests of the Western Region. In September 2024, Henry Okum entered into a verbal arrangement directly with Bernard Antwi Boasiako to enter the concession. Months later, on April 16, 2025, the national police service embarked on a coordinated special operation aimed at rooting out illegal mining activities that were destroying water bodies and forest reserves.</p><p><br>​The following day, police arrested Michael Gyadu Ayisi and twenty-eight other men in the Samreboi Forest, operating within a concession belonging to Akonta Mining Company Limited. At the time of the sudden arrest, the team was occupying a temporary structure bearing the mining company&#39;s name.</p><p><br>​A thorough police search retrieved a massive cache of items from the illegal miners. Law enforcement confiscated eight pump-action guns, one single-barrelled gun, 310 cartridges of ammunition, and four sharp machetes. They also recovered 25 serviceable heavy excavators, four non-serviceable excavators, heavy-duty water pumping machines, one grease gun, four motorcycles, two vehicles, five pieces of metal suspected of being gold concealed in a sachet, and 157,000 Ghanaian Cedis in cash.</p><p><strong><br>​The Chain of Command and Charges</strong><br>​During rigorous interrogation, Michael Gyadu Ayisi identified Henry Okum as his direct employer, assigned to supervise the lucrative mining activities. Ayisi claimed his team was simultaneously undertaking land reclamation on devastated parts of the concession while actively mining untouched portions. When tracked down, Henry Okum revealed that he had entered into a verbal arrangement directly with Bernard Antwi Boasiako, under the impression the concession belonged entirely to the political figure.</p><p><br>​On October 7, 2025, Bernard Antwi Boasiako and his company were formally arraigned before the High Court. The Republic presented a comprehensive charge sheet containing six distinct counts of criminal behavior. </p><p>Count one addressed the illegal assignment of mineral rights without approval, contrary to the Minerals and Mining Act 2006 and its 2019 Amendment. The offense stated he purposely permitted the mining operation without the mandatory prior written approval of the Minister responsible for Lands and Natural Resources. </p><p>Count four further charged him with purposely facilitating an unlicenced mining operation.<br><em><strong>​Legal Standards and The Defense Strategy</strong></em><br>​During adjudication, the trial judge anchored her decision in centuries of established common law tradition regarding the heavy burden of proof. </p><p>The court cited the 1935 case of Woolmington versus DPP to affirm that the prosecution must prove the guilt of the prisoner. Referencing the 1961 case of Commissioner of Police versus Antwi, the judge noted there is absolutely no burden on the accused to prove anything; their only burden is to raise a reasonable doubt.</p><p> Finally, drawing from the 1947 case of Miller versus Minister of Pensions, she explained that proof beyond a reasonable doubt need not reach absolute mathematical certainty but must carry a highly probable degree of certainty.</p><p><br>​The defense team, led by attorney Samuel Atta Akyea, mounted a rigorous legal defense. They argued the mining laws were fundamentally unconstitutional for severe vagueness and overbreadth, requesting a referral to the Supreme Court. </p><p>The trial judge firmly refused, ruling the laws were explicitly clear. Furthermore, using the rule of statutory interpretation, the attorney argued that the phrase regarding dealing in mineral rights only applied to formal written transactions, not verbal permission.</p><p> The court rejected this, noting it would allow wealthy concession holders to bypass state regulations entirely through unwritten agreements.</p><p><br><strong>​The Factual Dispute Between Reclamation and Mining</strong><br>​The central factual dispute revolved around the exact nature of the verbal permission. </p><p>Bernard Antwi Boasiako freely admitted permitting Henry Okum to enter the land, but maintained it was exclusively for agricultural land reclamation, specifically the planting of coconut seedlings on the already mined and degraded portions of the concession. He testified that he did not grant permission to conduct any mining operations.</p><p><br>​The Republic dismantled this defense using circumstantial evidence and basic economic logic. Land reclamation is an incredibly expensive engineering endeavor, yet Boasiako advanced absolutely no financial resources to Okum. Henry Okum, a licenced small-scale miner rather than an agricultural expert, testified under oath that he was permitted to mine on the unmined portions of the concession to defray the cost of reclamation.</p><p> The court logically concluded that it completely defies fundamental economic logic for a seasoned miner to deploy millions of Cedis worth of heavy machinery deep into a remote forest simply to plant coconut trees without direct financial compensation.</p><p><br><strong>​Piercing the Corporate Veil and Sentencing</strong><br>​The defense attempted to rely on the Companies Act to argue that Akonta Mining Company Limited was a separate artificial entity and the individual director could not be held personally liable. </p><p>However, the court decisively lifted the corporate veil due to a shocking absence of basic corporate governance, including no board meetings, no official corporate documents, and an absent second director. Henry Okum&#39;s testimony confirmed that on the ground, the company and the individual were treated as one and the same.</p><p><br>​Upon establishing guilt, the court handed down an incredibly severe sentence. </p><p>Bernard Antwi Boasiako was sentenced to twenty years imprisonment in hard labor and a fine of 10,000 penalty units. Akonta Mining Company Limited was fined 30,000 penalty units, and all expensive confiscated machinery was permanently forfeited to the State.</p><p><strong><br>​Political Fallout and Selective Justice Allegations</strong><br>​The political reaction was immediate and explosive. The New Patriotic Party convened an emergency press conference, led by General Secretary Justin Kodua Frimpong, framing the conviction as malicious political persecution orchestrated by the National Democratic Congress government.</p><p><br>​The party directly alleged selective justice, demanding to know the status of public allegations against high-ranking NDC figures. They specifically named Joseph Yamin and Abanga Yakubu, who were subjects of a formal EOCO petition regarding illegal mining. They also pointed to Members of Parliament Mohammed Yakubu and Oscar Ofori Larbi, both of whom faced heavy public accusations of mining involvements from traditional leaders. </p><p>Concluding the press conference, the party publicly declared Chairman Wontumi a political prisoner.<br>​<strong>The Socioeconomic Tragedy</strong><br>​While grave political allegations demand serious investigation, they do not erase the undeniable facts established in the courtroom. </p><p>The rule of law strictly requires that every citizen be held accountable for environmental destruction.<br>​However, the execution of modern justice must also deeply consider the broader socioeconomic impact. </p><p>A sentence of two solid decades in hard labor is exceptionally strict for a first-time offender who serves as the executive leader of a vast chain of legitimate businesses spanning national media, logistics, and real estate.</p><p><br>​By incarcerating him for twenty continuous years, the court has effectively signed a devastating death warrant for these businesses. The true, tragic victims of this harsh sentence are the thousands of completely innocent employees who rely on these companies for their daily wages. These hardworking citizens, who had nothing to do with the illegal mining in the Samreboi Forest, now face sudden unemployment.</p><p><br>​True justice should carefully balance strict retribution with economic restoration. </p><p>A far more intelligent approach would have been to impose astronomical financial penalties to fully fund the environmental restoration of the devastated lands, combined with a much shorter custodial sentence or strict regulatory probation. </p><p>Jailing him for twenty years destroys countless lives and livelihoods, ultimately turning a legal victory into a harsh, unforgiving economic tragedy</p>",
