      {
        "title": "Going Forward Nicaragua Will No Longer Hold Elections Pres Daniel Ortega Officially Declares",
        "slug": "going-forward-nicaragua-will-no-longer-hold-elections-pres-daniel-ortega-officially-declares",
        "category": "General news",
        "publishedDate": "2026-07-22",
        "readTime": "2m read",
        "summary": "Central America is experiencing a massive political shock. Nicaragua has officially abandoned any remaining pretense of democratic governance. President Daniel Ortega, who is 80 years old, has explicitly declared that his country is done holding elections. The announcement effectively cancels the upcoming November 2027 general election. This move permanently cements the absolute control he shares with his wife and co president, Rosario Murillo. Here is a breakdown of how Ortega is locking out the opposition and what this means for the region.",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>Central America has just experienced a massive political shock. Dropping any remaining pretense of a democratic process, President Daniel Ortega of Nicaragua has explicitly declared that his country will no longer hold elections.<br></p><img src=\"https://eu-west-2.graphassets.com/cmrmsa7zm00ij01wc3pwrgjxy/cmrwff1aq4y4r06mnp81b798g\" alt=\"image.png\" title=\"image.png\" width=\"1407\" height=\"703\" /><p>He dropped the bombshell on Sunday evening in the capital city of Managua. He was speaking to a massive crowd of state employees to celebrate the 47th anniversary of the 1979 Sandinista Revolution. This was the very movement Ortega helped lead to topple a dictatorship previously backed by the United States.</p><p>It was his first public appearance in over two months, as he had not been seen in public for 61 days. During this monumental address, he certainly did not hold back.</p><p>Ortega told the cheering crowd of state workers and loyalists that there will be no more elections so that the opposition can try to seize the government and seize power. He frequently accuses opposition parties of being American puppets. He added that the days when parties backed by foreign powers would return to power are completely over.</p><h2><em>Building a Wall Against the Opposition</em></h2><p>Canceling the vote is just the first step. Ortega also laid out a clear strategy to legally and permanently shut out his political rivals. He promised to work closely with the National Assembly to pass strict new laws that will secure his grip on the government.<br></p><img src=\"https://eu-west-2.graphassets.com/cmrmsa7zm00ij01wc3pwrgjxy/cmrwfi0d254io07meok6z27th\" alt=\"000_18V9NW.jpg\" title=\"000_18V9NW.jpg\" width=\"2736\" height=\"3840\" /><p>These laws, he explained, are meant to build a wall and a barrier against the coup plotters and the traitors who sell out their country. For context, the phrase coup plotters is the preferred label Ortega uses for the citizens and political rivals who took part in the massive 2018 anti government protests.</p><h2><em>The Death of the 2027 Elections</em></h2><p>The most immediate casualty of this move is the next general election in Nicaragua.</p><p>Voters were originally supposed to head to the polls in November 2026. However, constitutional tweaks made earlier pushed the expected election to November 2027. Now, thanks to the recent announcement, that timeline is entirely dead in the water. The country was scheduled to hold general elections next year, but the electoral system has been functionally abolished.<br></p><img src=\"https://eu-west-2.graphassets.com/cmrmsa7zm00ij01wc3pwrgjxy/cmrwfjj6u556n07mel79nhmfa\" alt=\"image.png\" title=\"image.png\" width=\"722\" height=\"389\" /><h2><em>Consolidating a Family Dynasty</em></h2><p>For human rights groups and international watchdogs, this declaration is not exactly a surprise. It is simply the final nail in the coffin for a democracy that has been suffocating for years.</p><p>Ortega has been in power continuously since 2007. Over the last decade, he has systematically torn down democratic structures. Things really came to a head during the highly controversial 2021 elections. He secured a fourth straight term only after his government banned parties and threw every major opposition candidate behind bars.</p><p>On top of that, constitutional changes did not just give him more time in office. They also elevated his wife, Rosario Murillo, from Vice President to a wildly powerful new role as co president. Together, the couple now holds absolute control over the laws, courts, and government bodies of the country, effectively turning Nicaragua into a family run state.</p><h2><em>Global Backlash</em></h2><p>The international reaction has been sharp and immediate. Tiziano Breda, an analyst for the Armed Conflict Location and Event Data project, noted that while the reality on the ground has been shifting for a while, the rhetoric has now officially escalated.</p><p>Breda pointed out that with this decision, Ortega and Murillo have sealed the transition to a full fledged family dictatorship.</p><p>The United States State Department under Secretary of State Marco Rubio had already released a press statement condemning the move. Rubio stated that the declaration lays bare the true authoritarian nature of the family dictatorship. He noted that the leadership has abandoned even the pretense of popular consent. By officially slamming the ballot boxes shut, Ortega has stripped away the last remaining illusion of democracy in Nicaragua, ensuring that he and his family rule unchallenged for the foreseeable future.</p>",
