                    "text": "\n​In a clear and direct response, the office of the flagbearer made it unequivocally known that Dr Bawumia had absolutely no involvement in this legal action. The statement emphasized that he neither lodged a formal police complaint nor gave his blessing for the prosecution of this individual or any other citizen.\n​A Commitment to Democratic Free Speech\n​Addressing the nature of political discourse in Ghana, the release pointed out that Dr Bawumia has endured insults and falsehoods throughout his entire public life. Despite these frequent attacks, he has never resorted to using the instruments of state power to silence his critics. He assured the public that he has no intention of starting such a practice now.\n​According to the statement, his core conviction is that the best response to offensive speech is better speech. Similarly, he believes the appropriate answer to falsehoods is simply the truth. He categorically rejected the use of handcuffs, courtrooms, and crushing bail conditions to punish ordinary Ghanaians for their words.\n​Calling Off the Prosecution\n​Taking decisive action, Dr Bawumia respectfully urged both the Ghana Police Service and the Attorney General to halt the current legal proceedings. He requested that they discontinue this specific prosecution as well as any similar actions that security agencies might be pursuing on his behalf. His primary request is for the authorities to release the young man so he can return to his family and resume his daily livelihood.\n​The press release concluded on a unifying note about the health of the nation. Dr Bawumia stated that the democracy of Ghana is only strengthened when all citizens have the freedom to speak without fear, including those who actively oppose the ruling party.\n​Showing grace toward his detractor, the flagbearer publicly forgave the young man and wished him well. Finally, he used the opportunity to urge all political actors across the divide to channel their energy toward solving the real issues that matter to the Ghanaian people."
