        "category": "General news",
        "publishedDate": "2026-07-23",
        "readTime": "2m read",
        "summary": "The Accra High Court has thrown out a legal challenge against the National Democratic Congress (NDC)'s directive requiring ministers and government appointees to resign from their offices at least six months before contesting the party's internal elections.",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>The Accra High Court has thrown out a legal challenge against the National Democratic Congress (NDC)&#39;s directive requiring ministers and government appointees to resign from their offices at least six months before contesting the party&#39;s internal elections.</p><p><br>The case sought to overturn the party&#39;s decision, arguing against the validity of the directive. However, after considering the matter, the court dismissed the suit, effectively allowing the NDC&#39;s rule to remain in force.</p><p><br>The directive forms part of the party&#39;s internal regulations governing leadership contests. According to the NDC, requiring government appointees to step down well before internal elections is intended to promote fairness and create a level playing field for all aspirants.</p><p><br>Supporters of the policy argue that it prevents incumbents from using the influence, resources, or visibility associated with public office to gain an unfair advantage during party contests. They also believe it strengthens transparency and reinforces confidence in the party&#39;s internal democratic processes.</p><p><br>The directive has, however, generated debate within political circles. </p><p>While some see it as a necessary measure to protect the integrity of internal elections, others believe it could discourage capable public officials from seeking party leadership positions due to the risks associated with resigning months before an election.</p><p><br>With the court dismissing the challenge, the NDC is expected to continue enforcing the directive ahead of future internal elections unless it is amended by the party or overturned through a successful appeal.</p><p><br>The ruling is likely to influence preparations for upcoming party contests, as ministers and government appointees considering parliamentary or executive positions within the NDC will now have to comply with the existing resignation requirement.</p><p><br>Political observers say the d