      {
        "title": "Government Plans to Tax Netflix, Starlink and Other Cross-Border Digital Services",
        "slug": "government-plans-to-tax-netflix-starlink-and-other-cross-border-digital-services",
        "category": "General news",
        "publishedDate": "2026-07-26",
        "readTime": "2m read.",
        "summary": "The Government is preparing to introduce a tax framework for cross-border digital services such as Netflix, Starlink, and other international online platforms. Deputy Finance Minister Thomas Nyarko Ampem says the move is aimed at ensuring foreign digital service providers contribute fairly to Ghana's tax revenue.",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>The Government has announced plans to introduce a tax regime targeting cross-border digital services, a move that could affect popular platforms such as Netflix, Starlink, and other international subscription-based services used by Ghanaians.<br></p><img src=\"https://eu-west-2.graphassets.com/cmrmsa7zm00ij01wc3pwrgjxy/cms23vbb9imui07l1y3lofcn6\" alt=\"images (9).jpeg\" title=\"images (9).jpeg\" width=\"596\" height=\"335\" /><p><br>Speaking on the proposal, Deputy Finance Minister Thomas Nyarko Ampem said the increasing use of foreign digital platforms has created the need for a system that ensures these companies contribute their fair share of taxes to Ghana&#39;s economy.<br><br>According to him, millions of Ghanaians now rely on international digital services for entertainment, internet connectivity, cloud computing, software subscriptions, and other online activities. However, many of these companies generate revenue from Ghana without having a significant physical presence in the country.<br><br>The proposed tax framework is expected to align Ghana with a growing global trend, where governments are introducing measures to tax multinational digital companies that earn income from local users. Authorities argue that such policies promote tax fairness by ensuring both local and foreign businesses operate under comparable tax obligations.<br></p><img src=\"https://eu-west-2.graphassets.com/cmrmsa7zm00ij01wc3pwrgjxy/cms23wgv7irtg07miatg7sm2a\" alt=\"images (10).jpeg\" title=\"images (10).jpeg\" width=\"739\" height=\"415\" /><p><br>While the government believes the initiative could increase domestic revenue and strengthen public finances, the proposal is also likely to spark debate among consumers and businesses. Some observers have expressed concern that digital service providers could pass any additional tax costs on to customers through higher subscription fees.<br><br>The announcement comes as Ghana continues to explore new sources of revenue to support public spending and adapt its tax system to the rapidly expanding digital economy. Officials maintain that the objective is not to discourage innovation or digital adoption but to ensure the tax system keeps pace with changing patterns of commerce.<br><br>Further details, including which services will be affected, the proposed tax rates, and the timeline for implementation, are expected to be announced as government develops the new framework.<br><br>Published by Orpheus Grant-Essilfie</p>",
