                    "text": "\nSpeaking on the proposal, Deputy Finance Minister Thomas Nyarko Ampem said the increasing use of foreign digital platforms has created the need for a system that ensures these companies contribute their fair share of taxes to Ghana's economy.\n\nAccording to him, millions of Ghanaians now rely on international digital services for entertainment, internet connectivity, cloud computing, software subscriptions, and other online activities. However, many of these companies generate revenue from Ghana without having a significant physical presence in the country.\n\nThe proposed tax framework is expected to align Ghana with a growing global trend, where governments are introducing measures to tax multinational digital companies that earn income from local users. Authorities argue that such policies promote tax fairness by ensuring both local and foreign businesses operate under comparable tax obligations.\n"
