      {
        "title": "Ghana to Deploy Condom Vending Machines Nationwide by End of 2026 to Strengthen HIV Prevention",
        "slug": "ghana-to-deploy-condom-vending-machines-nationwide-by-end-of-2026-to-strengthen-hiv-prevention",
        "category": "General news ",
        "publishedDate": "2026-07-27",
        "readTime": "2m read",
        "summary": "The Ghana AIDS Commission (GAC) has announced plans to install condom vending machines across the country by the end of 2026 as part of efforts to reduce new HIV infections and improve access to sexual health services. The initiative is expected to make condoms more accessible, reduce the stigma associated with purchasing them, and strengthen the country's HIV prevention strategy.",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>Ghana is set to take another significant step in its fight against HIV/AIDS with the nationwide deployment of condom vending machines by the end of 2026, according to the Ghana AIDS Commission (GAC).<br><br>The machines will be installed at strategic public locations, including transport terminals, shopping centres, tertiary institutions, entertainment venues, and other high-traffic areas. By making condoms available around the clock, health authorities hope to remove barriers that discourage people from practising safe sex.<br><br>The initiative comes at a time when Ghana continues to make progress in reducing HIV infections but still faces a considerable public health challenge. Current estimates indicate that more than 337,000 people are living with HIV in the country. Although new infections have declined in recent years, nearly 14,000 new cases are still recorded annually, underscoring the need for stronger prevention measures.<br><br>Health officials estimate that dozens of people contract HIV every day in Ghana. Young people remain among the most vulnerable, with individuals between the ages of 15 and 24 accounting for a significant proportion of new infections. Women also continue to be disproportionately affected, representing nearly two-thirds of people living with the virus.<br><br>The Ghana AIDS Commission believes expanding access to condoms remains one of the most effective ways to reduce HIV transmission, alongside regular testing, early treatment, comprehensive sexual health education, and increased public awareness. Officials argue that making condoms readily available through vending machines could encourage more people to adopt safer sexual practices.<br><br>Another key objective of the programme is to tackle the social stigma associated with buying condoms. Many people, particularly young adults, avoid purchasing them because they fear being judged. The vending machines are expected to provide a private, convenient, and discreet alternative, helping to increase condom use without embarrassment.<br><br>The initiative forms part of Ghana&#39;s broader strategy to end AIDS as a public health threat by 2030. In addition to improving access to condoms, the government continues to invest in HIV testing, treatment services, awareness campaigns, and community outreach programmes aimed at reducing new infections and ensuring more people know their HIV status.<br><br>Public health experts have welcomed the proposal, describing it as a practical intervention that complements existing HIV prevention efforts. However, they caution that condom vending machines alone will not eliminate the disease. They stress that sustained education, behavioural change, expanded healthcare services, and community engagement will remain essential to achieving long-term success in the fight against HIV/AIDS.<br><br>If successfully implemented, the nationwide rollout could become one of Ghana&#39;s most significant public health initiatives in recent years, improving access to preventive healthcare while supporting the country&#39;s efforts to reduce HIV transmission and protect future generations.<br></p>",
          "raw": {
            "children": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Ghana is set to take another significant step in its fight against HIV/AIDS with the nationwide deployment of condom vending machines by the end of 2026, according to the Ghana AIDS Commission (GAC).\n\nThe machines will be installed at strategic public locations, including transport terminals, shopping centres, tertiary institutions, entertainment venues, and other high-traffic areas. By making condoms available around the clock, health authorities hope to remove barriers that discourage people from practising safe sex.\n\nThe initiative comes at a time when Ghana continues to make progress in reducing HIV infections but still faces a considerable public health challenge. Current estimates indicate that more than 337,000 people are living with HIV in the country. Although new infections have declined in recent years, nearly 14,000 new cases are still recorded annually, underscoring the need for stronger prevention measures.\n\nHealth officials estimate that dozens of people contract HIV every day in Ghana. Young people remain among the most vulnerable, with individuals between the ages of 15 and 24 accounting for a significant proportion of new infections. Women also continue to be disproportionately affected, representing nearly two-thirds of people living with the virus.\n\nThe Ghana AIDS Commission believes expanding access to condoms remains one of the most effective ways to reduce HIV transmission, alongside regular testing, early treatment, comprehensive sexual health education, and increased public awareness. Officials argue that making condoms readily available through vending machines could encourage more people to adopt safer sexual practices.\n\nAnother key objective of the programme is to tackle the social stigma associated with buying condoms. Many people, particularly young adults, avoid purchasing them because they fear being judged. The vending machines are expected to provide a private, convenient, and discreet alternative, helping to increase condom use without embarrassment.\n\nThe initiative forms part of Ghana's broader strategy to end AIDS as a public health threat by 2030. In addition to improving access to condoms, the government continues to invest in HIV testing, treatment services, awareness campaigns, and community outreach programmes aimed at reducing new infections and ensuring more people know their HIV status.\n\nPublic health experts have welcomed the proposal, describing it as a practical intervention that complements existing HIV prevention efforts. However, they caution that condom vending machines alone will not eliminate the disease. They stress that sustained education, behavioural change, expanded healthcare services, and community engagement will remain essential to achieving long-term success in the fight against HIV/AIDS.\n\nIf successfully implemented, the nationwide rollout could become one of Ghana's most significant public health initiatives in recent years, improving access to preventive healthcare while supporting the country's efforts to reduce HIV transmission and protect future generations.\n"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        "image": {
          "url": "https://eu-west-2.graphassets.com/cmrmsa7zm00ij01wc3pwrgjxy/cms31t020ufff07l1bwkz2u1s"
        }
      },
      {
        "title": "Techiman Krobo Nursing Training College Students Protest Over Alleged Poor Food Quality",
        "slug": "techiman-krobo-nursing-training-college-students-protest-over-alleged-poor-food-quality",
        "category": "General news",
        "publishedDate": "2026-07-28",
        "readTime": "2m read",
        "summary": "Students of the Krobo Nursing and Midwifery Training College in the Techiman North District have staged a protest over what they describe as poor food quality and deteriorating living conditions. Images of meals allegedly served at the college have sparked widespread public debate, with students demanding improvements in their welfare and the removal of the ",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>Students of the Krobo Nursing and Midwifery Training College have taken to the streets to protest against what they describe as poor feeding, inadequate welfare, and administrative challenges at the institution.</p><img src=\"https://eu-west-2.graphassets.com/cmrmsa7zm00ij01wc3pwrgjxy/cms4xoft9d45y07mg64dis1s0\" alt=\"1785259840480.jpg\" title=\"1785259840480.jpg\" width=\"810\" height=\"810\" /><img src=\"https://eu-west-2.graphassets.com/cmrmsa7zm00ij01wc3pwrgjxy/cms4xnb6xczs407mqkyxj6s2y\" alt=\"1785259843825.jpg\" title=\"1785259843825.jpg\" width=\"810\" height=\"810\" /><p><br>The demonstration followed growing dissatisfaction among students, who claim that the meals served at the college are of poor quality and insufficient despite the fees they pay for feeding. Images circulating on social media, reportedly showing some of the meals provided to students, have intensified public discussion about conditions at the institution.</p><p><br>Beyond concerns about food, the protesting students have raised several additional grievances. They allege that they have been deprived of essential learning materials they paid for, including textbooks and medical equipment. </p><p>They also complained about the cancellation of breakfast, limited access to potable water, and inadequate library services, arguing that these issues are affecting both their health and academic performance.</p><p><br>According to the students, repeated attempts to have their concerns addressed through internal channels yielded little response, prompting them to stage the demonstration. </p><p>They submitted a petition calling for the removal or transfer of the college&#39;s principal, accusing the administration of poor leadership, lack of transparency, and mismanagement.</p><p><br>The protest attracted a heavy security presence as police officers were deployed to maintain law and order while students marched peacefully and presented their grievances to the relevant authorities.</p><p><br>The incident has reignited national discussions about student welfare in Ghana&#39;s nursing and midwifery training institutions. </p><p>Many observers have called on the Ministry of Health and other relevant authorities to investigate the allegations and ensure that students receive nutritious meals, adequate learning resources, and a conducive environment for their training.</p><p><br>As of the time of writing, the college&#39;s management had not publicly responded in detail to the specific allegations made by the students. However, many stakeholders are urging dialogue between management and the student body to resolve the issues and restore normal academic activities.</p><p><br>The protest highlights the growing importance of student welfare in tertiary institutions, particularly those responsible for training the country&#39;s future healthcare professionals. </p><p>Many believe that ensuring quality nutrition and a supportive learning environment is essential to producing competent nurses and midwives capable of delivering quality healthcare across Ghana.</p>",
