      {
        "title": "Supreme Court Rules Against Delegate System in Political Party Primaries",
        "slug": "supreme-court-rules-against-delegate-system-in-political-party-primaries",
        "category": "General news",
        "publishedDate": "2026-07-29",
        "readTime": "3m read",
        "summary": "In a landmark 5 to 2 majority decision, Ghana's Supreme Court has ruled that political parties can no longer rely on the delegate system to elect their presidential and parliamentary candidates. Instead, all registered party members in good standing must be allowed to vote in internal elections, a decision that could significantly reshape the country's political landscape",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>Ghana&#39;s Supreme Court has delivered a historic judgment that is expected to transform the way political parties conduct their internal elections.<br><br>In a 5 to 2 majority ruling, the Court held that the delegate system used by political parties to elect presidential and parliamentary candidates should no longer be used. The judges ruled that every registered member of a political party who is in good standing must be given the opportunity to vote in these internal contests.<br><br>For many years, Ghana&#39;s major political parties, including the New Patriotic Party (NPP) and the National Democratic Congress (NDC), have selected their flagbearers and parliamentary candidates through a delegate system. Under this arrangement, only selected party officials and delegates were permitted to vote on behalf of the wider membership.<br><br>The Supreme Court found that this approach does not fully reflect the democratic principles enshrined in Ghana&#39;s Constitution. As a result, political parties will now have to allow their ordinary members to participate directly in choosing parliamentary candidates and presidential flagbearers.<br><br>The ruling is expected to bring significant changes to Ghana&#39;s political system. Political parties will have to amend their constitutions, revise their electoral guidelines, and update their voter registers to comply with the Court&#39;s decision. Future internal elections are also expected to involve far more participants than in previous years.<br><br>Many political observers believe the judgment will strengthen internal democracy by giving grassroots members a stronger voice in deciding who leads their parties. They argue that it will reduce the influence of a small group of delegates and make the selection process more transparent and representative.<br><br>At the same time, the decision presents new challenges. Organising nationwide primaries involving all registered members will require greater financial resources, stronger logistics, and improved electoral systems. Political parties will also need to educate their members on the new voting process once the ruling takes effect.<br><br>The Supreme Court is expected to release its full written judgment in the coming weeks, providing detailed legal reasons for its decision and guidance on how political parties should implement the new system.<br><br>The ruling is already being described as one of the most important constitutional decisions affecting Ghana&#39;s democratic process since the country returned to multi-party rule in 1992. It is expected to reshape internal party elections and expand the role of ordinary members in selecting the nation&#39;s future political leaders.<br><br></p>",
          "raw": {
