        "content": {
          "html": "<p>The <strong>Krobo Nursing and Midwifery Training College</strong> has been shut down indefinitely following the recent student protest over poor feeding and welfare conditions.</p><p>The closure comes after tensions escalated on campus during demonstrations, with reports indicating that some students were arrested while others allegedly sustained injuries during an operation by security personnel to restore order. The developments sparked widespread public concern and renewed calls for a peaceful resolution to the crisis.</p><p>In response, the <strong>Ministry of Health</strong> stepped in to address the situation. Acting on behalf of the Ministry, officials engaged the <strong>Techiman Municipal Chief Executive (MCE)</strong> and officers from the <strong>Criminal Investigations Department (CID)</strong> to meet with students and the school&#39;s management.</p><p>The meeting, which lasted several hours, sought to de-escalate tensions, listen to the concerns raised by students, and find a lasting solution to the dispute. Students had earlier complained about the quality of meals served on campus, inadequate welfare, and other conditions they believed were affecting their academic life.</p><p>Despite the extensive discussions, authorities ultimately decided to close the institution until further notice. The temporary shutdown is intended to restore calm, ensure the safety of students and staff, and create an environment where the issues raised can be addressed without further disruption.</p><p>Students have been directed to vacate the campus while the Ministry of Health and other stakeholders continue their assessment of the situation. It is expected that further engagements will take place before a decision is made on when academic activities can safely resume.</p><p>The closure marks a significant development in a dispute that has attracted national attention and reignited conversations about student welfare in Ghana&#39;s nursing and midwifery training institutions. Many stakeholders are now urging dialogue, transparency, and meaningful reforms to prevent similar incidents in the future.</p><p><strong>Published by Orpheus Grant-Essilfie</strong></p><p><br></p>",
