        "content": {
          "html": "<p>Currency interventions by central banks are not unusual. They are often used to ease sharp swings in exchange rates, calm market uncertainty, and ensure businesses can access foreign currency when demand suddenly rises. In Ghana&#39;s case, the IMF believes those interventions played an important role in supporting macroeconomic stability as the country worked through its economic recovery programme.<br></p><img src=\"https://eu-west-2.graphassets.com/cmrmsa7zm00ij01wc3pwrgjxy/cmshakwucke0b07mn2f5bz5ix\" alt=\"shutterstock_2159962175.webp\" title=\"shutterstock_2159962175.webp\" width=\"820\" height=\"437\" /><p><br>At the same time, the Fund argues that such large-scale support cannot become a permanent strategy. A market that depends too heavily on central bank intervention may struggle to reflect the true forces of supply and demand. Over time, that can discourage a more competitive and transparent foreign exchange market.<br><br>Rather than criticizing the interventions outright, the IMF&#39;s assessment strikes a balance. It acknowledges that the Bank of Ghana acted during an exceptional period, while encouraging a gradual shift toward a system where the market plays a greater role in determining exchange rates and the central bank intervenes only when necessary.<br><br>The Fund also welcomed the Bank of Ghana&#39;s efforts to introduce a more structured foreign exchange operations framework. A clearer set of rules, it noted, can make interventions more predictable, improve transparency, and strengthen confidence among investors and market participants.<br><br>The disclosure comes as Ghana continues to implement reforms under its IMF-supported programme. Inflation has eased compared with previous highs, foreign exchange reserves have improved, and confidence in the economy has gradually strengthened. Even so, the IMF maintains that sustaining these gains will depend on disciplined monetary policy, prudent fiscal management, and a foreign exchange market that becomes increasingly resilient without heavy and continuous central bank support.</p>",
