        "title": "Free Speech Under Siege: Martin Kpebu Warns Government Over Rising Arrests",
        "slug": "free-speech-under-siege-martin-kpebu-warns-government-over-rising-arrests",
        "category": "Politics",
        "publishedDate": "2026-08-10",
        "readTime": "2m Read",
        "summary": "Lawyer Martin Kpebu is speaking out against the recent wave of free speech arrests in Ghana, noting that the Mahama government has made more arrests in 18 months than the previous administration did in eight years.",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>Private legal practitioner Martin Kpebu is very upset about the current situation. He recently went on television to share his frustration about the growing number of citizens being locked up just for expressing their opinions.  </p><p></p><p>Kpebu pointed out a very shocking comparison. He stated that the current Mahama administration has arrested more people over free speech in just 18 months than former President Akufo-Addo did during his entire eight years in office.  </p><p></p><p>He reminded everyone that during the previous government, citizens openly called leaders harsh names without being thrown into a police cell. But today, the climate is completely different.  </p><p></p><p>A perfect example of this shift is the arrest of the popular Ghanaian social media influencer known on X (formerly Twitter) as Gen Buhari. Known for being a vocal supporter of the opposition and sharing his strong political opinions online, his arrest yesterday has shocked many internet users.  </p><p></p><p>Sadly, his story is part of a growing trend where the police are targeting social media users and political communicators.</p><p>Just a few months ago, an opposition party communicator named Baba Amando was arrested. He was put in handcuffs and driven for eight hours simply because he posted some digitally altered pictures on Facebook.  </p><p></p><p>Another social media user, a TikTok creator named Camilla Alhassan, was also tracked down by the police and remanded in custody just for making claims about the president in a video.  </p><p></p><p>Martin Kpebu says this new strategy of using the police to silence critics is completely wrong. While he agrees that insulting leaders is not a polite thing to do, he firmly believes that arresting citizens is never the right solution.  </p><p></p><p>His advice to the government is very clear: just let the people talk.</p><p>He warned that if the government keeps arresting citizens for speaking their minds, they will quickly lose all the goodwill and support of the people.  </p><p></p><p>At the end of the day, harsh words, internet memes, and social media posts do not actually hurt the government. But locking people up for talking definitely hurts Ghana&#39;s democracy.</p>",
