        "content": {
          "html": "<p>According to new research from the Council for Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR), the numbers are quite shocking. Ghana is currently spending over $230 million every single year on fresh and processed tomato imports.</p><p><br>​Dr. Michael Kwabena Osei, a top researcher at the CSIR, explained that Ghanaians eat a lot of tomatoes. </p><p>The country needs over 1.4 million metric tonnes of tomatoes every year, but local farmers are only able to grow about 15 to 18 percent of that massive amount.<br>​Because of this huge gap, Ghana has to buy fresh tomatoes from neighboring Burkina Faso and rocessed tomato paste from places like China and Europe.</p><p><br>​The story is even worse when it comes to ginger. Right now, Ghana is 99 percent dependent on imported ginger just to meet the needs of its people.</p><p><br>​Just a few years ago, local farmers were growing plenty of it. But in 2022, a terrible plant disease known as the &quot;ginger killer&quot; wiped out almost all of the ginger farms.</p><p><br>​This bacterial outbreak caused local ginger production to drop from over 100 tonnes down to just 10 tonnes.<br>​Because ginger is so scarce now, the price has skyrocketed. A sack of ginger that used to cost 250 Ghana Cedis in 2022 recently jumped to around 6,000 Cedis.</p><p><br>​So, how can the country fix this expensive problem? The scientists say Ghana needs to invest heavily in its own farming technology.</p><p><br>​Dr. Osei warned that a country that imports all its seeds will always have to import its food. </p><p>He suggested creating a special Tomato Board and producing better, disease-resistant seeds for local farmers to plant.</p><p><br>​By focusing on better agricultural science, good irrigation, and healthier soils, researchers believe Ghana can start growing enough food to feed itself and keep millions of dollars inside the country.</p>",
