        "summary": "The Attorney General has officially withdrawn a motion at the Court of Appeal that aimed to stop former MASLOC CEO Sedina Tamakloe from being released. The legal fight has now moved to the Supreme Court.",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>​The legal drama surrounding former MASLOC CEO Sedina Tamakloe just took another interesting turn this morning.</p><p>​If you have been following the news, you know that the Court of Appeal recently shocked the nation by overturning her 10-year prison sentence and clearing her of all charges.</p><p>​Immediately after she was cleared, the Attorney General&#39;s office tried to stop her from going free. They filed a legal request at the Court of Appeal called a &quot;stay of execution&quot; to keep her in prison while they prepared to appeal the final decision.</p><p>​However, there is a major breaking update today, August 12, 2026. The Attorney General has officially changed their legal strategy.</p><p>​Principal State Attorney Hilda Craig appeared before the Court of Appeal this morning and completely withdrew their application to block her release. Because the government withdrew the request, the court officially struck the motion out.</p><p>​But this does not mean the government is giving up on the case. They are just moving the fight to a higher level.</p><p>​Instead of arguing at the Court of Appeal, the Attorney General has now taken the battle straight to the highest court in the land. They have filed a brand-new stay of execution directly at the Supreme Court.</p><p>​The state is still hoping to keep her behind bars while they fight to get her original 10-year sentence restored. The Supreme Court is scheduled to hear this new motion on October 13, 2026.</p><p>​For now, the battle lines have moved, and all eyes will be on the Supreme Court this October to see what happens next.</p>",
