      {
        "title": "\"We Came to Learn, Not to Sleep\": Ghanaian Students Protest Massive Spike in Hostel Prices",
        "slug": "we-came-to-learn-not-to-sleep-ghanaian-students-protest-massive-spike-in-hostel-prices",
        "category": "World",
        "publishedDate": "2026-08-13",
        "readTime": "2m Read",
        "summary": " Students across Ghana are heavily protesting the skyrocketing prices of private hostels. The movement is being led by organizations like NUGS and student groups from KNUST's Katanga Hall, and is now gaining official support, with the Unity Hall Master recently backing the students' demands for fair accommodation fees.",
        "content": {
          "html": "<p>University students across Ghana are standing up and demanding action. The cost of living around campus has become completely unaffordable.</p><p>Over the past few weeks, there has been a massive spike in the prices of private student hostels. The situation has gotten so bad that many students say they are now forced to pay much more for a simple bed than they actually pay for their university tuition.</p><p>This is not just an isolated problem. Almost every university in Ghana is facing the exact same issue.</p><p>At the University of Ghana, the prices are getting out of hand. For example, official rental rates for the 2026 academic year show that the cost for a single room at a major private alumni-built facility has jumped by massive, unmanageable amounts. Even a simple bed in a shared four-person room now costs a fortune.</p><p>Because of these crazy price hikes, the Acting Rent Commissioner, Frederick Opoku, recently issued an official directive freezing any planned private hostel fee hikes for the 2026/2027 academic year until further notice. He even held a special meeting with the Vice-Chancellor of the University of Cape Coast (UCC), Prof. Denis Worlanyo Aheto, to discuss ways to enforce rent laws and protect students from these unfair, arbitrary charges.</p><p>To make matters worse, students at UCC are dealing with even more financial surprises. Student bodies have raised serious alarms about both school and accommodation fees being increased without any prior notice or proper consultation. Students often return for the new semester only to discover they owe much more money than they planned for, leaving them scrambling to find the extra funds at the last minute.</p><p>After receiving numerous complaints about these arbitrary price jumps, the UCC Students&#39; Representative Council (SRC) President, Kwame Ntiamoah Ntim, officially directed all students to completely ignore and refuse to pay any unapproved hostel rent increases.</p><p>The anger recently boiled over at the Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology (KNUST). During the National Youth Conference held in Kumasi, members of the famous University Hall, widely known as Katanga, decided they had enough.</p><p>The Katanga morale group staged a peaceful but powerful protest right at the conference. They held up large signs with messages like &#39;Reduce hostel fees now!&#39; and &#39;We came to learn, not to sleep&#39;.</p><p>Obrane, a final-year Agricultural Engineering student and the leader of the morale group, spoke up for the crowd. He explained that these extreme prices are putting a terrible financial strain on parents who just want to see their children get a good education.</p><p>Student activist Selma Yoda recently went on national television to explain exactly how bad the living conditions are. She pointed out that at certain private complexes in Kumasi, rent has completely doubled, even though students still deal with water shortages, unpainted rooms, and broken facilities. The prices have become so severe that one female student was even forced to sleep in a church far from campus because she could not afford a room.</p><p>The National Union of Ghana Students (NUGS) is also taking the fight directly to the authorities. NUGS President Rashid Ibrahim Esq. formally petitioned the Rent Control Department, pointing out that some private landlords are breaking the law by forcing students to pay for multiple years of rent in advance just to secure a room.</p><p>Their complaints are actually working. Following recent inspections, the Rent Control Commission and the Ghana Tourism Authority officially shut down two specific private facilities in Koforidua. They were closed because officials found poor sanitation and unacceptable living conditions, proving students were paying high prices for terrible rooms.</p><p>The Rent Control Department also recently began conducting surprise inspections at private hostels around the University of Professional Studies, Accra (UPSA) after students there complained about extreme, exploitative fees.</p><p>Housing advocates, like the National Tenants Union of Ghana, are warning the government that this is a full-blown housing crisis. They argue that the outdated Rent Act of 1963 needs to be overhauled because the institutions meant to protect citizens simply lack the resources to enforce the law.</p><p>The government is starting to step in to find a nationwide solution. George Opare Addo, the Minister for Youth Development and Empowerment, was at the KNUST conference and saw the protest. He publicly promised to take the issue straight to the Ministry of Education and the Rent Control Department.</p><p>He also shared some good news for the future. The government is currently talking to private developers to build more affordable housing directly on university campuses as part of a new &#39;Bed for All&#39; agenda.</p><p>As the new school year gets closer, students all over the country are hoping these protests and official crackdowns will finally force landlords to bring their prices back down to a reasonable level.</p>",
